  "unit": "Unit 9 - Inequalities",
  "description": "Comprehensive lesson covering Unit 9 - Inequalities for SAT Math preparation.",
  "duration": 45,
  "content": "<p>Rules are similar to solving equations, with <strong>one key difference</strong>:<br />\n- When multiplying or dividing both sides by a <strong>negative number</strong>, the inequality sign <strong>flips</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Example 1</strong><br />\nSolve: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></math>  </p>\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>18</mn><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>\n\n<p><strong>Example 2</strong><br />\nSolve: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></math>  </p>\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>4</mn><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>\n\n<hr />\n\n<p>Translate carefully from words to math.</p>\n<p><strong>Example 3</strong><br />\nA concert ticket costs \\<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>\\</mi></mrow></math>250. How many tickets can you buy?</p>\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>40</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>250</mn><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>6.25</mn></mrow></math>\n\n<p>So maximum whole tickets = 6.</p>\n<p><strong>Example 4</strong><br />\nA test requires at least 70 points to pass. You currently have 42 points out of 60. How many of the remaining 40 points must you get?</p>\n<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> = points you score on last part.  </p>\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>42</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>70</mn><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>28</mn></mrow></math>\n\n<p>So you must earn at least 28 points.</p>\n<hr />\n\n<ul>\n<li>For one-variable inequalities:  </li>\n<li><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> → open circle at 3, shade left.  </li>\n<li>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> → closed circle at -2, shade right.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>For two-variable inequalities:  </p>\n</li>\n<li>Example: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> → graph line <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Shade <strong>below</strong> because of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>.  </li>\n<li>Use dashed line for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003C;</mo></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003E;</mo></mrow></math>; solid line for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example 5</strong><br />\nGraph solution set of:</p>\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>\n\n<p>Line: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, slope <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, intercept <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Dashed line. Shade region <strong>above</strong>.</p>\n<hr />\n\n<p>The solution is the <strong>overlap region</strong> of all shaded areas.</p>\n<p><strong>Example 6</strong><br />\nGraph and solve:</p>\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>\n\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>\n\n<p>The solution region is the intersection of the two shaded half-planes.</p>\n<hr />\n\n<ul>\n<li>Always flip the inequality when multiplying/dividing by negative.  </li>\n<li>Check <strong>boundary points</strong> (does equality hold?).  </li>\n<li>For word problems, highlight key phrases like “at most,” “at least,” “no more than.”  </li>\n<li>Graph shading is common on SAT - test with a sample point like <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n\n<p>1) Solve: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>17</mn></mrow></math><br />\n2) Solve: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math><br />\n3) A taxi charges \\<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>\\</mi></mrow></math>2 per mile. You have at most \\$30. How many miles can you ride?<br />\n4) Solve and graph: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math><br />\n5) System of inequalities:  </p>\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>\n\n<hr />\n\n<p>1) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>17</mn><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<p>2) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<p>3) Cost equation: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>26</mn><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></math>. So up to 13 miles.  </p>\n<p>4) Line: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, slope <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, intercept 3. Solid line (since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>). Shade below.  </p>\n<p>5) Graph both: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> (above dashed line), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> (below solid line). Solution = overlap region.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">\n</code></pre>",
